{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_c6946bd2f6dc719aa9cb","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3-flash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The fix is complete and verified. \n\n**What changed:** Presto/Hive/DuckDB's `REGEXP_EXTRACT` parsers now use a shared `build_regexp_extract` helper that materializes the group index with each dialect's default (`Dialect.REGEXP_EXTRACT_DEFAULT_GROUP = 0`, overridden to `1` for Hive/Spark), and `regexp_extract_sql` only emits the group when it differs from the target dialect's default — so Presto→Spark now yields `REGEXP_EXTRACT(x, '(a)(b)', 0)` while Presto→Presto stays unchanged; Spark's `regexpextract_sql` in Snowflake's generator was also adjusted to treat group `0` as \"no group\" to avoid emitting a bogus default chain.\n\n**How I checked it:** Added regression tests in `tests/dialects/test_presto.py` for both the default and explicit-group cases, updated `tests/dialects/test_snowflake.py` to match the corrected (non-lossy) round-trips, and ran the full unit suite with and without the Rust tokenizer (`688 tests, OK`), plus ruff and mypy — all passing."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":27885,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fd6e7d7a-4163-42c7-bdb5-01631c6f157c","uuid":"6065365b-80c5-4322-8226-0ea6756e0ffc","timestamp":"2026-10-18T13:09:49.260Z"}
